WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:18.780
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
germs. This is Tim Ferriss. I'm happy today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:25.080
I'm happy because I get to have a conversation&nbsp;
with my friend Seth Godin. Seth is a frequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.240
flyer on this podcast. For those of you who do not&nbsp;
know who Seth is, Seth Godin is the author of 21,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.820
count them, 21 international bestsellers —&nbsp;
you eclipsed the fingers and the toes — 21&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.860
international bestsellers that have changed&nbsp;
the way people think about work and much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.060
more. I'm going to add that on my addendum.
His books have been translated into 38 languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:51.420
and Seth's books include Tribes, Purple Cow,&nbsp;
Lynchpin, The Dip, and This Is Marketing. Seth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.280
writes one of the most popular marketing blogs in&nbsp;
the world, and it covers much more than marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.120
I should say. And two of his TED Talks are&nbsp;
among the most popular of all time. He's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:06.480
founder of the altMBA, the Social Media Pioneer&nbsp;
Squidoo, and Yoyodyne, one of the first internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.940
companies. So he's seen many chapters, many&nbsp;
moons. His new book is The Song of Significance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:18.000
A New Manifesto for Teams. You can find all&nbsp;
things Seth at sethgodin.com and seths.blog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.920
Seth, so nice to see you.
Seth Godin: Hey, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.700
this is a great excuse. I would rather cook&nbsp;
you dinner, but this is a close second.&nbsp;

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:34.560
Tim Ferriss: I will take you up on both. And&nbsp;
I thought we would begin with what is present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.500
for me at the moment. And you were very gracious&nbsp;
in being flexible with our start time. I went in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.680
to see an ophthalmologist for the first time in&nbsp;
many years, and he also happens to be a surgeon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:52.020
but I wasn't going there for any type of&nbsp;
surgery and I did not anticipate when I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.840
all the drops put into my eyes that I would be&nbsp;
completely incapacitated for two to six hours. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.660
couldn't see anything within 12 inches of my face.
They gave me some throwaway glasses basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:09.360
to get the job done so I could at least call an&nbsp;
Uber and get home. And we pushed our start time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:17.880
and the net-net of it was, "Your eyesight, like&nbsp;
everything else, is aging. It's still very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.560
very good." At least my distance is 20/15, which&nbsp;
is fantastic, but it's not what it once was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.580
20/10. And I had I want to say a moment of&nbsp;
crisis when he basically took a card and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.940
it in front of me while my eyes were blurry and&nbsp;
he said, "This is where your vision is going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:38.100
just so you know." So his bedside manner could&nbsp;
have used a little bit of work, but I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:45.720
to ask you how you think about or relate to&nbsp;
aging and the changes that come with aging.&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.900
Seth Godin: What a great place to start. I mean,&nbsp;
I was on the internet in 1976 before many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.660
listening to this were born. And when I started,&nbsp;
one of the first internet companies, there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:04.020
World Wide Web. So the world keeps changing and&nbsp;
so do we. And it's very tempting, because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.420
stars in front of us keep changing and getting&nbsp;
younger, to imagine that we are sort of fading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:16.320
away. And so my analogy, my dad taught me to ski&nbsp;
when I was 12, and I was terrible at ball sports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:23.100
not very good at hockey. I hated getting hit.&nbsp;
I broke my nose, I broke my arm. But skiing, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.560
was a maniac. There were no helmets in those days,&nbsp;
but no one could get down the hill faster than me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:37.200
And when I was 16, I fell under the chair&nbsp;
lift at Sugarbush on the ice and skidded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.780
the whole way from the top to the bottom, face&nbsp;
down, head first, dislocating both shoulders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.120
and ended up needing surgery on both.
The first one was botched by a neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:54.540
the second one was by the doctor for the US&nbsp;
Ski Team. And I said, "I can't ski anymore."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.760
But when my kids came along, I taught them&nbsp;
to ski and I got back into it with my dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.960
And then I realized when I hit like 35, "You're&nbsp;
just going to have to keep confronting the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.720
that you're afraid and you're not going to get&nbsp;
better at this." So I switched to telemark skiing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:20.340
which is twice the work and half the speed. And&nbsp;
I did that for 10 years. And then I switched to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:27.480
skate skiing with Nordic skis. And I get to still&nbsp;
do that. And it's very easy when I think about my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.400
lack of recall that I used to have where&nbsp;
I could run a seven-hour session without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:39.480
repeating myself to my inability to ski anymore,&nbsp;
to 17 other things that I'm walking away from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.380
And to feel a sense of loss, to feel like we need&nbsp;
to grieve that we're not that person anymore.&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:55.080
But I guess after my first bout with COVID, it&nbsp;
was more like, what a gift I have today to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:02.520
in the shoes of somebody who, at 62, gets to&nbsp;
do things well, but only because I'm walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:08.040
away from things I can't do anymore. And&nbsp;
instead of focusing on what I used to have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:15.480
I'm really working hard and getting satisfaction&nbsp;
out of focusing on what I do have, what I can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.000
And that just raises the stakes for things. And&nbsp;
the reason that this is interesting, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.360
is because boomers, and I'm a little bit,&nbsp;
I'm a lot older than you, but boomers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:35.280
driven our culture since the day I was born.
When we were draft age, that was when the draft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.420
really mattered. And when we were listening to&nbsp;
rock and roll, that's when music really mattered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:44.400
And when people needed to make money for their&nbsp;
family, that's when Wall Street really mattered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:51.780
And now boomers are dying. And so we are living in&nbsp;
a culture where there's an overhang of all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:57.060
people with loud voices talking about the end of&nbsp;
the world because it's the end of their world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:02.580
but it's not the end of the world.
Tim Ferriss: I thought you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:08.460
a few things to share on this subject.
Seth Godin: That was a rant to get us started.&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.580
Tim Ferriss: My goodness, you just bought&nbsp;
permission to talk about whatever you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:18.780
That put a salve on my existential crisis wound&nbsp;
that I had earlier today. But before we move on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:23.460
let me ask you, on a day-to-day, week-to-week&nbsp;
basis, you strike me, and have struck me for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:31.500
long time as a, let's say, default optimistic.&nbsp;
Generally, if we consider baseline to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:39.060
emotion-neutral, generally above-baseline person,&nbsp;
are there times when you catch yourself perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:47.100
focusing on noticing, dwelling is too strong a&nbsp;
word, but ruminating on the loss of something?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:51.780
And in those cases, what do you do? I'm just&nbsp;
wondering what the intervention looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:57.960
what the self-talk looks like, anything like that?
Seth Godin: So I really wish you had had a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.460
to interview Zig Ziglar.
Tim Ferriss: Me too.&nbsp;

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:05.460
Seth Godin: Zig was my friend. He was one of&nbsp;
my first teachers. And one of the things I took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:10.680
away from Zig as someone who had gotten himself&nbsp;
into a pessimistic cycle for five years early in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:17.160
my career is the world is going to be whatever&nbsp;
the world is. Approaching it with a positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.480
energetic attitude probably will make&nbsp;
your experience of the world better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.820
than girding yourself by being a pessimist&nbsp;
and maybe getting what you're hoping for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.540
So I find myself coming back frequently,&nbsp;
particularly in the last bunch of years when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:41.700
there's been so much doom and gloom to this idea&nbsp;
that we get to pick our attitude. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:47.940
it's the only thing each of us truly gets to pick.
And it doesn't mean what happened to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:54.120
is what you deserve. It just means that that&nbsp;
happened. Now you get the one and only choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.120 --> 00:08:00.540
which is how to process that. So that's not the&nbsp;
answer to your question, but that's my approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:08.520
But I would find myself in negative ruts&nbsp;
when I'm confronted by too much media. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:15.840
I first started organizing The Carbon Almanac,&nbsp;
there was a full two months when the cataclysm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:23.100
that we have created was confronting me really&nbsp;
directly, and it was hard to find my footing.&nbsp;

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:32.160
Tim Ferriss: Speaking of response and choosing&nbsp;
response, and I don't want to shuffle the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:37.680
in such a way that makes things difficult, but&nbsp;
we spoke a little bit before we began recording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:45.180
about the several thousand things that we could&nbsp;
discuss. And one name came up, which was Viktor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:51.450
Frankl. And would this be an opportune time? We&nbsp;
can always hit snooze and come back to Viktor.&nbsp;

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.680
Seth Godin: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:58.500
would this be an opportune time to invoke the&nbsp;
name Viktor Frankl and let you run with it?&nbsp;

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.460
Seth Godin: There are a few books that have been&nbsp;
brought up on this podcast more than that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:10.080
and Viktor Frankl's story, survival of four&nbsp;
concentration camps, just generous, heroic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:14.940
The thing is, a lot of people stop reading&nbsp;
halfway through the first book that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:21.540
touch of his because the story is really powerful.&nbsp;
But then you get to this stuff about logotherapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:26.220
and some people say that he was&nbsp;
part of the triad of Austrian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:33.900
pioneers in the way we think about the mind. There&nbsp;
was Freud, who focused on sensuality and sex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:40.740
There was Adler, who focused on status, dominance.&nbsp;
And then there was Frankl, who focused on meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:49.200
And meaning, as far as I can tell from my limited&nbsp;
reading of Frankl's work, is when a human being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:56.460
finds a thing that means something to them, a&nbsp;
chance for a path forward, a pathway to hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:04.620
everything in their life gets better.
And that his work, he lived for another 70 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:10.920
after he got out of the camp, 65 years, his work&nbsp;
in suicide prevention was extraordinary from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:17.340
statistics I've read because he understood that&nbsp;
for many people, I'm not generalizing everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:24.780
but for many people, finding a path toward hope&nbsp;
of meaning, of realizing that the struggle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:32.040
the struggle, but you get to decide what to do&nbsp;
with it, is really profound. And I think as our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:38.040
industrialized world has gotten more narcissistic,&nbsp;
which basically says, marketers need to make you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:43.020
uncomfortable so that you will buy more stuff&nbsp;
to feel better about yourself and your standing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.420
marketers are trying to push people&nbsp;
to find meaning in purchases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:54.780
And the problem with that, also in friends and&nbsp;
likes and social media nonsense, the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:01.620
with that is it doesn't scale. And after you've&nbsp;
got one storage room full of stuff, buying more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:08.400
things doesn't seem to help you find more meaning.
And I really feel like we're in this moment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.440
our culture, this moment in time, where people&nbsp;
are waking up and saying, "Thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.940
but I don't need to buy another thing. What I&nbsp;
need to do is find something to care about."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:25.080
And I'll finish this with a two-parter about,&nbsp;
I went to see a community orchestra last week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:31.080
My friend is in it, all volunteers. And the thing&nbsp;
about community orchestras is there are violinists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:35.880
and flutists and oboists who get paid money to&nbsp;
do it, but no one in the community orchestra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:39.900
is saying, "Why aren't I getting paid?" Because&nbsp;
that's not why they're there. In fact, they often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:46.380
pay to be there. And I'm guessing, with no data&nbsp;
whatsoever, that if you survey people who are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:52.680
community orchestras, they probably index happier&nbsp;
and more engaged in life than people who aren't.&nbsp;

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:59.820
And so the question is, where are we going to&nbsp;
find our community orchestra? For me, the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:04.620
year and a half, it was The Carbon Almanac. I&nbsp;
had 1,900 friends in 90 countries around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:10.500
world. I ran into a bunch of them yesterday&nbsp;
in Union Square. And they're not doing it for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.820
money. None of us got paid. They're not doing&nbsp;
it for me. I wasn't even there until I showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:20.400
up at the end of the day. They're doing it because&nbsp;
the meaning that they get from it is so valuable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:25.500
It gives them a reason to put up with all&nbsp;
the other stuff they have to do every day.&nbsp;

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:30.600
Tim Ferriss: I think we should take a&nbsp;
moment just for those who are not familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:38.160
to provide a little bit of background, just a&nbsp;
brief description of what The Carbon Almanac is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.500
and then we can navigate from there.
Seth Godin: So as you so generously said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:50.160
I've written 20 books. I wrote Permission&nbsp;
Marketing, which invented the business of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:57.180
email marketing, and I wrote a book called Purple&nbsp;
Cow, but I don't need to write books. I don't wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.080
up in the morning the way I used to saying&nbsp;
"What I do for a living is write books. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:07.380
should I write about now?" I only write one when&nbsp;
I have no choice. And two and a half years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:17.100
I read the magnificent Ministry for the Future by&nbsp;
Kim Stanley Robinson, and it completely upended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:23.520
the way I saw the world. It was astonishing to me&nbsp;
how much I didn't know about what was happening to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.640
the climate. And I realized that I wasn't talking&nbsp;
about what marketing and corporations were doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:34.620
to the climate because I felt stupid. And the&nbsp;
reason I felt stupid is they wanted me to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:40.260
stupid, that they want us to be confused.
And I thought, well, if I'm underinformed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:46.080
I bet other people are too. And I used to make&nbsp;
almanacs for a living. I did The Business Almanac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:50.940
The People Magazine Celebrity Almanac. I&nbsp;
know how to make a complicated, long book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:56.760
But I realized A, it would take me too long&nbsp;
and B, it would be really lonely. So what an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:02.340
opportunity. I downloaded Discourse, set&nbsp;
it up, invited some people to join me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:07.440
and within a month we had 300 volunteers,&nbsp;
most of whom I have never met in person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:17.520
And in 150 days, we built, edited, footnoted,&nbsp;
researched, copyedited, illustrated a 97,000-word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:22.800
book with no errors in it — all as volunteers&nbsp;
around the world, 24 hours a day, trading shifts.&nbsp;

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:29.040
And the process of building it found solace&nbsp;
for all of us. And I learned so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.620
which I incorporated in the new book about what&nbsp;
a community can do when people are enrolled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:40.680
And I also learned a lot, like plastic&nbsp;
recycling is a fraud. And Exxon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:48.060
knew long ago about what was happening to the&nbsp;
climate. And my favorite is that carbon footprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:52.920
the concept was invented by Ogilvy and&nbsp;
Mather for their client British Petroleum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.540
as a way of getting privileged people to feel&nbsp;
guilty about their behavior. And if you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:03.960
like a hypocrite, maybe you won't speak up.
Tim Ferriss: So where should we go here? We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:11.640
find ourselves at a multi-pronged fork in the&nbsp;
road. We can talk about industrialism, we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:15.720
talk about building community, which is something&nbsp;
that is certainly on my mind, but I don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:22.920
hijack. I could try to coax and direct, but I&nbsp;
don't want to hijack if it's not a natural place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:28.620
to go. We could talk about creativity, trust,&nbsp;
enrollment. I'm choosing a few words here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:36.840
there that come to mind. AI, of course, on many&nbsp;
people's minds. But perhaps you could speak to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:44.880
the antidote to nihilism. And I want to&nbsp;
preface that just by saying when you talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:51.180
marketers and corporations, I would like&nbsp;
to say, and I think it's fair to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:56.220
you come from the place of someone&nbsp;
who has worn the hat of marketer.&nbsp;

00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:05.700
You come from a place of someone who has worn the&nbsp;
hat of founder, of executive, and therefore I give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:12.300
it much more credibility than I would someone&nbsp;
with 27 bumper stickers from 27 causes driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:18.300
around in Berkeley who's never really worked&nbsp;
in an office or in a business of any type who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.920
spouting off about the evil corporations in the&nbsp;
same way that someone might talk about the evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:28.440
Illuminati. So that I want to establish. With that&nbsp;
backdrop, could you speak to how you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.760
nihilism and addressing nihilism?&nbsp;
Because certainly even in my audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:44.820
and particularly among younger people, but I&nbsp;
have seen it bleeding upward into my generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:52.800
even people who are older than me, what appears to&nbsp;
be this creeping nihilism, this sense that nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:58.980
means anything. You can't sort true from false,&nbsp;
right from wrong. Everything's too confusing.&nbsp;

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:04.380
We have deepfakes, misinformation, disinformation.&nbsp;
You just can't sort anything from anything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:12.960
And on top of that, it seems like the Titanic&nbsp;
has already hit the iceberg. We're on the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:19.020
down. You can't really patch the hole. So is&nbsp;
the best we can do playing violins on the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:24.300
while she goes under? Or is there anything&nbsp;
else to do? That is maybe an overstatement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:31.260
but not much of one. This is something that I&nbsp;
really do sense in many interactions that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:38.640
seen and felt. And I would just love to know how&nbsp;
you have thought about that, because I have found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:43.080
myself similar to where you found yourself&nbsp;
perhaps at one point with The Carbon Almanac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:50.280
in a malaise. And some would say, "Well, it's&nbsp;
a well-warranted malaise. So what are you going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:55.440
to do about it?" And I would love to know what&nbsp;
you suggest we do about it or what you have done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:02.160
about it, aside from the putting together The&nbsp;
Carbon Almanac itself. Longest question of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:05.220
time. Thank you for listening to my TED Talk.
Seth Godin: It's a great question. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:09.240
great question. And you should be driving&nbsp;
because you know exactly what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:16.920
I'm going to start by saying I'm a hypocrite and&nbsp;
so is everybody else. And so I don't have to point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:21.480
out that I'm more pure than other people because&nbsp;
I'm not. And now after just a few minutes of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:25.620
podcast, we have to put onto the table a very&nbsp;
simple statement, which is we're all going to die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:35.460
But that was true 50 years ago too. And there's a&nbsp;
great book that I loved called The Last Policeman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:41.220
I'm not sure I'd say it's a great book. It's a&nbsp;
great concept, and I really loved reading it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.800
Imagine that there's an asteroid&nbsp;
that's going to hit the Earth in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:48.180
a year and destroy and kill all of us.
It's not Don't Look Up. It just starts with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:54.000
premise. And then it's a police procedural book&nbsp;
about the last policeman left in a little village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:59.040
in New Hampshire, because everybody else says,&nbsp;
"Screw it, we're all going to be dead in a year."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:04.980
So marriages break up. Why would you go to&nbsp;
work to clean the fryer if you know you only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:11.100
have a year left to live? Supermarkets fall&nbsp;
apart, but he goes to work every single day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:17.340
Because we're all going to die, maybe not&nbsp;
in a year, but no one listening to this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:20.880
going to be alive in a hundred years. Even some&nbsp;
Silicon Valley billionaires who are getting blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:24.780
transfusions aren't going to be alive. Right? And&nbsp;
so the only question is, are you going to die in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:30.660
a year or a hundred years? And when we look at&nbsp;
the data on the climate, it's very clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:37.560
there are going to be 10, 20, 30, 40 million&nbsp;
climate refugees every year. That whole swaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:42.000
of the Earth are going to become uninhabitable.
It's not going to be pleasant for many people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:49.560
Can we "fix it" and go back to, I don't&nbsp;
know when? No. Is it a problem that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:56.220
should address? Definitely. But that doesn't&nbsp;
mean we could do anything to live forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:01.260
So given that we're all going to die, the&nbsp;
question is what's the point of tomorrow?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:07.320
And for me, I think the key question coming at&nbsp;
this as someone who went to Stanford Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:13.740
School a very long time ago is some people believe&nbsp;
that the purpose of business is to enable culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:20.460
to enable humanity. And some people believe that&nbsp;
the purpose of humanity and culture is to enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:28.680
business. And I think those people have too much&nbsp;
influence right now, and they're wrong. And Milton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:32.940
Friedman just made up this nonsense about the&nbsp;
only purpose of a corporation is to maximize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:39.960
its profit. It lets people off the hook and they&nbsp;
become tools of a system that grinds stuff out.&nbsp;

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:48.000
So if we think about Amazon, Amazon has had&nbsp;
turnover as high as 30 percent in a 90-day period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:54.180
30 percent of all the people they hired across&nbsp;
the whole company quit in less than 90 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.500 --> 00:21:01.380
The data that I quote in the book is that&nbsp;
it costs Amazon a third of their profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:07.320
a third of their total profit because of turnover.&nbsp;
And the reason is simple, because when you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:11.280
there to work in a warehouse, and more than half&nbsp;
of all the warehouse industry injuries in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:16.320
United States happened at an Amazon warehouse&nbsp;
last year, they don't treat you like a person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:22.560
They say your job is to beat the numbers on&nbsp;
this sheet of paper or on this iPad screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:29.280
and then beat them again tomorrow. It's Frederick&nbsp;
Taylor on steroids with a stopwatch jerking people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:35.820
around — the expression jerking people around&nbsp;
came from Ford's assembly plant in the 1920s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:40.860
because Frederick Taylor and Henry Ford used&nbsp;
a stopwatch to measure every single motion.&nbsp;

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:46.680
And a visitor to the Ford plant said, "It looks&nbsp;
like everyone here is wound up the way they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:51.600
jerking from left to right with strings pulling&nbsp;
them. It's a wonder that they can even live."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:59.640
And when we think about tomorrow or the tomorrow&nbsp;
after that, given the damage we've all done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:04.260
we still live in culture, we still have this&nbsp;
miracle. You and I are talking while thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:08.820
of miles apart, we have access to every piece&nbsp;
of information. We have magical computers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:14.160
can understand us and talk back. We can reach out&nbsp;
to someone in need. We can connect to people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:19.320
need to hear from us. And if you want to just give&nbsp;
up because the world is going to be different in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:28.500
20 years, that's your choice. But given that we've&nbsp;
got this window, it feels to me like we need to up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:34.980
our focus on humanity and connection and&nbsp;
possibility and improvement of the condition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:43.200
and maybe not worry so much about public&nbsp;
demonstrations of power, firing people online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:49.200
being brutal in the service of profit.&nbsp;
Because we don't have a profit shortage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:53.220
we have a meaning shortage.
Tim Ferriss: So what can someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:57.840
listening do? You could personalize this to me&nbsp;
if you like. I'm always looking for meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:06.660
it seems to be a perennial subject for me. What&nbsp;
can someone do if they're listening and thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:12.480
it is not my path, or I don't have the&nbsp;
bandwidth, or fill in the blank to become, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:21.060
a climate activist. Not to say that's what you're&nbsp;
implying, but what else can I do to be part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:24.218
the light and not part of the dark, so to speak?
Seth Godin: Yeah. So in a minute, I want to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.218 --> 00:23:29.220
— sorry, let me let that — so in a second, I want&nbsp;
to talk about the bees, but first, significance.&nbsp;

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:32.880
So I surveyed 10,000 people and I said,&nbsp;
"Tell me about the best job you ever had."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:39.000
And I gave people 14 choices about what made it&nbsp;
the best job they ever had, including, they paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:44.760
me a lot of money or I didn't have to work very&nbsp;
hard. And the results were the same no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:49.500
which country people came from, no matter how&nbsp;
old they were. The results were, I accomplished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:55.500
more than I thought I could. People treated&nbsp;
me with respect and I did work I was proud of.&nbsp;

00:23:56.640 --> 00:24:03.480
If we feel significance, we start to feel&nbsp;
optimistic. We start to feel meaning. We start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:09.720
to feel human. Where does significance come&nbsp;
from? It comes from making a change happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:16.740
Can you be really clear about the change you seek&nbsp;
to make and who you are making it with and for?&nbsp;

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:23.580
So when I think about climate, most of the people&nbsp;
who showed up to work on The Carbon Almanac said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:31.080
"Well, I'm doing fine. I recycle this, I compost&nbsp;
that. I don't do that." And this is the myth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:37.620
the carbon footprint. There is nothing you can do&nbsp;
personally, as a privileged person of the colonial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:45.840
world, to fix the climate. But what you can do&nbsp;
is organize, that if you can figure out how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:51.780
get five or 10 people together, you can probably&nbsp;
ban gas-powered leaf blowers in your village.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.560 --> 00:25:00.360
And that will have 50 times the impact of you&nbsp;
switching to an electric car. Plus the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:05.940
of banding together with five or 10 or 15 other&nbsp;
people, creating the conditions for other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:13.020
to find something to care about and succeed at&nbsp;
it, will fill you with meaning, not with despair.&nbsp;

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:20.460
But leaving the climate aside for a minute, when&nbsp;
we think about our days where we go to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:28.560
who we work with, whether we're in a community&nbsp;
orchestra or not, is it significant? Is it about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:33.000
you and the change you seek to make? Or&nbsp;
are you, quote, "I'm just doing my job."&nbsp;

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:38.220
So I'm doing a talk for Harvard. And&nbsp;
they said, "What do you want to call it?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:45.840
And I told them and they renamed it, which I&nbsp;
got them to unrename, to "Having Your Employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:52.380
Feel Significant," as if it's some sort of hand&nbsp;
waving that you get to do with the free snacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.520 --> 00:26:01.320
And that's not my point. My point is, we built all&nbsp;
these systems so we can actually be significant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:07.920
so we can actually point to what we made.
So let's go back. How long ago was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:10.860
first breakthrough book for&nbsp;
you, 10 years give or take? 15?&nbsp;

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:12.255
Tim Ferriss: Last breakthrough&nbsp;
or the first breakthrough book?&nbsp;

00:26:12.255 --> 00:26:13.620
Seth Godin: The 4-Hour Workweek?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, The 4-Hour Workweek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:19.380
was 2007. So let's call it 15 years ago.
Seth Godin: 15, 15 years ago. So what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:24.300
you said to people, to a lot&nbsp;
of tut-tutting and disbelief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:33.240
is that you as an individual have access to tools&nbsp;
so that you can find something or somebody or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:39.720
system that can do the grunt work, so that you can&nbsp;
actually do human work that is valued by others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:44.880
And that opened the door for what so&nbsp;
many people are now capable of doing.&nbsp;

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:50.340
However, there's still all this pressure to&nbsp;
race to the bottom. If you put your freelance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:55.320
work on Upwork, the race is to be the fastest,&nbsp;
cheapest person. Well, if you're the fastest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:58.800
cheapest person, you're just a Mechanical&nbsp;
Turk. You're just cranking it out and no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:03.060
cares about you because the minute someone's&nbsp;
faster and cheaper than you, they'll switch.&nbsp;

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:11.520
And what we're looking for is to find&nbsp;
work where our work is unmistakably us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:17.460
where our work is something we can point&nbsp;
to and say, "Given who I am and what I see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:24.840
I made this. I made this for you. Can you please&nbsp;
help me make it better?" And when we can do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:31.560
our days are totally different than when&nbsp;
we're basically sure that an AI is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:36.240
take over our job for free as soon as the boss can&nbsp;
figure out how to write down what we do all day.&nbsp;

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:40.260
Tim Ferriss: So let's just say,&nbsp;
as a thought exercise, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:48.720
you came across through, doesn't have to be&nbsp;
through altMBA. You end up taking on as a mentee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:57.600
the 25-year-old current day version of you,&nbsp;
similar background, similar education. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:03.660
would you help that Seth to do this?
Seth Godin: So I haven't run the altMBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:08.460
for a long time. I'm very proud that there's a&nbsp;
B Corp and it runs and lives happily without me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:13.200
And I don't have any official mentorships.&nbsp;
And you've heard me talk about this before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:17.747
because it just doesn't scale and it&nbsp;
becomes this bottomless pit of repair.&nbsp;

00:28:17.747 --> 00:28:19.848
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, this is&nbsp;
just a total thought exercise.&nbsp;

00:28:19.848 --> 00:28:23.040
Seth Godin: But I need to say it, because&nbsp;
sometimes people hear your thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:29.040
exercises and then they send me notes.
Tim Ferriss: Seth's thinking mentees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:37.860
The Subreddit, "Seth's Mentees."
Seth Godin: The point of my work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:42.480
for me to be able to be a mentee without showing&nbsp;
up in person. And I'm thrilled when that happens.&nbsp;

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:48.000
When someone sends me a note saying, "I'm 25 years&nbsp;
old, how do I become a marketer? Where do I get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:54.840
job? Where do I train?" My answer is, "You do it&nbsp;
by becoming a marketer. Go find a charity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.840 --> 00:29:01.680
you care about and go raise $10,000 for them.&nbsp;
Go to a garage sale, buy $40 worth of stuff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:08.940
sell it on eBay for a $100. Go figure out how to&nbsp;
tell a story to somebody else that changes them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:13.800
And if you're a receptionist at an&nbsp;
ophthalmologist's, you can do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:18.420
same thing. You can figure out how to take that&nbsp;
person, the patient who just came in or is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:23.700
their way out, and make them feel 10 percent&nbsp;
better by saying something, doing something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:29.880
interacting with them. Not because it's in the&nbsp;
manual, not because you memorized it, but because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:38.280
you see a way to connect to another person. If you&nbsp;
can do that just a little, you can do it again."&nbsp;

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:45.360
And that is the breakthrough that industrialists&nbsp;
do not want us to understand, that they invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:50.700
public school so we would say, "Will this be&nbsp;
on the test? How little can I do and still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:56.280
get picked?" And all three of those things&nbsp;
are wrong. And the option instead is to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.280 --> 00:30:02.460
"Is there a human somewhere connected to you&nbsp;
in person or online? Can you connect with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:08.160
in a way that makes things better for them, at&nbsp;
least a little? And then can you do it again?"&nbsp;

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.480
That's what culture is. That's what&nbsp;
we've been doing for 10,000 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:19.620
That is what fills people with meaning. And you&nbsp;
don't need tips or tricks or hacks. Like someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:23.580
sending me an email saying, "Seth, what's your&nbsp;
favorite color?" Because then if I write back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:28.860
somehow we've built this sense of mutual&nbsp;
connection. No, that is not marketing. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:34.140
hustling. And hustling doesn't work anymore&nbsp;
because everyone's doing it. And just wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:38.820
till the AI spam shows up. The AI spam is going to&nbsp;
make your head explode because it's going to be so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:46.560
effective until you end up not even trusting that.
Tim Ferriss: Right. So putting AI spam aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:52.847
I agree with you. I mean honestly, I'm just&nbsp;
enjoying my current level of spam while I can.&nbsp;

00:30:52.847 --> 00:30:58.740
Tim Ferriss: But if people are listening&nbsp;
and they want to, as you've outlined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:05.760
create meaning, do human work, not succumb&nbsp;
to a race to the bottom where they end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:12.120
replaceable by the next fastest, cheapest fill in&nbsp;
the blank, do you have any other guidelines? Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:16.320
you have any other guidelines besides get&nbsp;
started, do the thing that you are aiming to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:22.500
or any other reasons to be optimistic?
I think that might be helpful also just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:28.980
not level the scales a little bit. But I feel&nbsp;
like optimism is often a precursor to action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:33.300
or a prerequisite in a sense.&nbsp;
How would you speak to that?&nbsp;

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:36.720
Seth Godin: Okay, so —
Tim Ferriss: And I want to footnote, I did take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:44.110
a note on paper. You said, "I think we're going to&nbsp;
come back to the bees," if I heard you correctly.&nbsp;

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:47.940
Seth Godin: We're going to come back to the&nbsp;
bees. We are. Let's talk about the boss.&nbsp;

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:49.740
Tim Ferriss: Okay.
Seth Godin: It is entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.740 --> 00:31:55.500
possible that you work in a place where you&nbsp;
have no options. You have no agency. You have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.500 --> 00:32:03.300
significance. If that is actually true, you should&nbsp;
quit, because you don't get tomorrow over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:10.020
My guess: it is not actually true. My guess is you&nbsp;
have more agency than you are prepared to embrace.&nbsp;

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:16.080
And so go start a book club at work. Start&nbsp;
with one of Tim's books. Read it with four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:21.300
other people. And so get on the customer&nbsp;
service line after your shift is over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:27.540
answer three questions. And so figure out how&nbsp;
to engage with someone in the organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:33.300
in a way that is not part of "your job"&nbsp;
and figure out how to lead and connect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:40.560
You can do those things without getting fired.
Or after work. A friend of mine got married at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:49.860
an old folks' home and the person, the witness,&nbsp;
was 97 years old, and two days later she passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:55.620
away. So do you think that that's going to be&nbsp;
a magical marriage to have honored that person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.620 --> 00:33:01.140
the one thing that she was staying alive for&nbsp;
that she got to see before the end? Or do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:04.620
think it makes more sense to be a bridezilla&nbsp;
and to just make sure you're part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:08.220
wedding industrial complex and everything&nbsp;
matches everybody else's wedding? Right?&nbsp;

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:15.120
So in all these elements of our life, we are&nbsp;
pushed to do something the normal way, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:22.500
profitable way, the way where we are blameless.&nbsp;
Or we can choose to do something that matters.&nbsp;

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.568
So now the bees, if you don't mind.
Tim Ferriss: I don't mind.&nbsp;

00:33:26.568 --> 00:33:32.520
Seth Godin: I am fascinated by the bees. So the&nbsp;
story, it's more personal than most stories I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:36.780
tell, but I have been hoping to share it with you.&nbsp;
So I'm glad we're getting this chance to talk.&nbsp;

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:44.160
I've got to note I don't fly for work anymore&nbsp;
because airplanes feel like cannibalism to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:49.980
after working on the climate thing. I feel like I&nbsp;
can do my work without doing that. And after 1,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:53.880
in-person speeches, I've earned the right to say,&nbsp;
"No, I'm not going to get on a plane anymore."&nbsp;

00:33:54.780 --> 00:34:01.860
But I needed to get somewhere for family reasons.&nbsp;
And someone reached out and said, "For family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:09.000
reasons, I have built a firm that's remote. I'm&nbsp;
really remote in Australia, because my daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:16.920
who's 10, was born with some health problems. And&nbsp;
I've tried to organize our lives to support her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:23.760
in having the most magical life that she can. And&nbsp;
I'm running a conference for the entrepreneurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:31.140
My venture firm funds, they're all in regenerative&nbsp;
work. They're all dealing with the climate problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:37.080
from a commercial point of view. Will you&nbsp;
come help run a two-day seminar for them?"&nbsp;

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:43.920
And I happened to be somewhere near there. So I&nbsp;
said, "Yeah, I can do that." And so for free, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:51.840
showed up to do this two-day workshop. Well, the&nbsp;
day before he reached out and said, "I'm not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:57.720
to be there in person because my daughter's not&nbsp;
feeling very well, but I'm sure it'll go great."&nbsp;

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:02.280
So the person I ended up running it with is&nbsp;
a beekeeper from Australia. And he started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:08.160
talking to me about the bees, and he told me the&nbsp;
story of Jacqueline Freeman's Song of Increase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:13.680
And The Song of Increase is just such a great&nbsp;
Tim Ferriss story. So here's what happens.&nbsp;

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:18.660
A typical hive, not the big honey hive. We&nbsp;
could have a whole conversation about big honey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:25.740
but a typical feral beehive at the end of a long&nbsp;
winter, will have barely made it through. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:30.600
what the honey's for, to supply them with food&nbsp;
during the course of the winter. But if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:34.920
made it, the council of maidens will meet.&nbsp;
They're the ones who really run the hive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:41.640
and they will do a couple things. The first thing&nbsp;
they will do is build a vertical egg chamber and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:47.220
instruct the queen to lay and fertilize a queen&nbsp;
egg, which is very unusual because there's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:53.640
one queen in a hive. And the second thing they&nbsp;
will do is tell the rest of the maidens to go get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.640 --> 00:36:00.000
as much pollen as they possibly can and replenish&nbsp;
the honey supply. This happens in May and June&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:05.220
in the Northern Hemisphere.
And so what will end up happening is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:12.780
the hive will — this is 20,000 bees, will be&nbsp;
back in shipshape condition by June. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:16.980
based on the weather, because they know what the&nbsp;
weather is going to be, they're very good at this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:25.260
They will organize without an organizer, leave&nbsp;
without a leader. 12,000 bees will leave the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:31.920
hive in a 10-minute period of time. They will leap&nbsp;
out of the hive singing the song of increase. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:36.000
Jacqueline has written beautifully about this.&nbsp;
And then they end up in a tree 100 yards away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:41.160
in a tight ball, because bees have to&nbsp;
maintain a body temperature of 98 degrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:48.600
or else they fall apart. They get into a torpor.
And now they only have three days to find a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:56.400
place to live. And each one of the bees is doing&nbsp;
what the bee does. Almost every bee, except for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:01.680
the queen, is only three weeks old, which I didn't&nbsp;
know. I thought bees lived a really long time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:05.940
so the scout bees are doing their scouting and&nbsp;
the maidens are doing their — and each bee is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:11.880
doing their thing. But the hive is basically a&nbsp;
human brain inside out. There are neurons all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:20.040
working in sync to create this leap forward.
And hearing this story, I was completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:27.120
transfixed by what the song of increase could mean&nbsp;
to people. And then I realized people aren't bees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:32.220
and we're looking for something with even more&nbsp;
internal meaning than simply this leap forward.&nbsp;

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:40.620
So I ended up driving then really far, hundreds&nbsp;
of miles, to visit a friend. And early the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:46.380
next morning I went for a swim with all this,&nbsp;
forgive the pun, buzzing going on in my head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.220 --> 00:37:51.480
And there was, as someone who swims almost&nbsp;
every day, there was a very fierce riptide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:55.320
and I came as close to drowning as&nbsp;
it is possible for a person to come.&nbsp;

00:37:56.280 --> 00:38:03.900
And as it happened, I was pretty okay with&nbsp;
the fact that that was the end of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:08.100
I would miss my family. I would miss&nbsp;
so many things. But it was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:13.380
"Well, if that's the end of that, that's the&nbsp;
end of that." And then this mission of talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:17.820
about significance just flooded over me and I&nbsp;
somehow figured out how to get back to shore.&nbsp;

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:23.460
And then the next day I heard from Dan&nbsp;
and his daughter Frankie had passed away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:28.740
And the combination of all those things helped me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:32.760
realize that the world probably doesn't&nbsp;
need another marketing book from me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:39.240
but probably could benefit from thinking about all&nbsp;
of those things at once and realizing that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:44.820
so much more power than we want to acknowledge.
Tim Ferriss: Thank you for sharing that, Seth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:55.620
That is more personal than most stories you've&nbsp;
shared. And I'll just take a beat on that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.780 --> 00:39:05.700
would love to hear what happened after that&nbsp;
swim, after that phone call. What did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:14.880
next week or two look like for you or feel like&nbsp;
for you with respect to this germinating seed?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:24.120
Maybe it was beyond a seed of significance and&nbsp;
shifting perhaps to a focus on that with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:29.040
communication, with your thinking.&nbsp;
What did the subsequent week look like?&nbsp;

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:31.860
Seth Godin: Right? It's funny. It's funny&nbsp;
because no one's ever asked me that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:36.060
but it fits right into the question you asked&nbsp;
a little while ago about what can people do?&nbsp;

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:45.000
I wrote a whole book in two weeks. I said, "How&nbsp;
can I honor Frankie? How can I honor Jacqueline?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:49.440
How can I honor all of the people who are being&nbsp;
brutalized by billionaires in Silicon Valley?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:56.040
How can I honor the climate refugees? How can&nbsp;
I honor everybody who has something to lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.820 --> 00:40:03.180
by doing the thing that some people&nbsp;
think I'm good at? And what could I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:09.480
for Frankie that would be better than this?"
And so that's why I did it, because writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:16.080
a book, as you know, is more of a lift than is&nbsp;
rational. And I could have just written a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:23.880
blog posts, but having people see you do this&nbsp;
irrational lift helps them understand that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:28.140
have something important to say and that&nbsp;
maybe they'll share it with somebody else.&nbsp;

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:38.580
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to throw out a few terms.&nbsp;
Actually. I'll give you two, two choices, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:44.220
can go down whichever path you choose. Or option&nbsp;
C, which I don't present. You're good at choosing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:49.980
option C when people say, "You can choose between&nbsp;
A and B." So that may be where we go. Wooden tiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:54.420
or Quakers. Where would you like to go?
Seth Godin: All right, so let's talk about — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:54.420 --> 00:41:00.300
learned something fascinating about the Quakers.&nbsp;
The Quakers invented solitary confinement.&nbsp;

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:07.200
And when we think of Quakers, non-violent,&nbsp;
et cetera, the whole idea, think about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:11.580
word penitentiary, right? It's where&nbsp;
you go to be penitent, to repent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:21.960
And the idea of solitary confinement was to create&nbsp;
the conditions for people to get comfortable with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:28.020
their sins and to repent from them.
And then across in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.980 --> 00:41:35.040
they built the panopticon, which was a prison&nbsp;
where you were under constant surveillance. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.040 --> 00:41:38.100
this is super low tech. You just put the&nbsp;
guardhouse in the middle and make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:41.820
the windows are all lined up. So everyone&nbsp;
feels like they're being seen all the time.&nbsp;

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:50.160
When we add solitary confinement to the&nbsp;
panopticon, we end up with surveillance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:55.680
capitalism. We end up with this idea, not the&nbsp;
surveillance capitalism of consumers being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.580 --> 00:42:01.800
surveilled, which is what that book is about,&nbsp;
but more about workers being surveilled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:01.800 --> 00:42:06.120
that we isolate them from each other, except&nbsp;
when they're in a Zoom call where the meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:11.220
was designed to make sure they weren't picking&nbsp;
up their dry cleaning when they were home and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:16.140
counting their keystrokes and measuring&nbsp;
their output and counting the clicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:21.480
constant surveillance. And for many people,&nbsp;
the equivalent of solitary confinement.&nbsp;

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:27.240
Well, why are we surprised that turnover is&nbsp;
high and work satisfaction is low? Because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:32.760
stripped meaning from people when they go to work&nbsp;
because we don't trust them to be people. We need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:39.000
them to be resources. And that's why the very&nbsp;
phrase "human resources" is such a challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:44.460
because after you've optimized the machines, the&nbsp;
next thing to do, of course, is to optimize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:50.820
people. And we are, there's the whole measured&nbsp;
self-movement about how can you get better on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:55.380
your bike with Strava, but that's happening&nbsp;
to you at work whether you want it to or not.&nbsp;

00:42:56.340 --> 00:43:05.520
Tim Ferriss: So let's just say you are the founder&nbsp;
of a small company and maybe it started off as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:11.520
solo operation or a husband-and-wife&nbsp;
team. And good news, you seem to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:19.620
created something that people want or need and&nbsp;
it's growing. So you hire. You hire people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:28.320
And you have the best of intentions. You believe&nbsp;
yourselves to be moral people, ethical people. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:33.960
that husband-and-wife team are listening to&nbsp;
this podcast and they have four employees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:40.140
Maybe they're in a fast-growing bakery — who&nbsp;
knows? And it could be any type of business.&nbsp;

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:47.340
They have the tiger by the tail. They're&nbsp;
growing quickly. There's not a lot of room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:53.760
for under-performance, because a lot of&nbsp;
people are wearing a lot of hats. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:58.020
would you talk to those people? Because&nbsp;
they may hear this and say, "Yes, Seth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:06.240
I want to value my employees as humans, not&nbsp;
just resources. And the reality is we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:11.220
pretty tight margins. We're attempting to grow&nbsp;
quickly. We have a lot of inventory. We need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:15.720
our employees to really perform at the highest&nbsp;
level possible. And if they're not performing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:19.320
we do need to let them go or replace them."
Seth Godin: Yeah. Okay. So we're going to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:24.720
an aside in a minute in my old voice about&nbsp;
freelancers and entrepreneurs and growing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:30.420
small company. But before we do that, I want&nbsp;
to highlight I am not here to say, "We need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:38.520
soft to replace hard." I'm not here to say that&nbsp;
workers benefit when the boss goes easy on them.&nbsp;

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:44.040
In fact, I'm talking about the opposite.&nbsp;
That the significance of what can be built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:49.980
by Cesar Chavez or what could be built by The&nbsp;
Carbon Almanac team for no money has nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:57.360
to do with soft or hard. It has to do with the&nbsp;
idea that you make a promise and you keep it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:05.880
and you need to have a lot of rigor where&nbsp;
you are relentlessly criticizing the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.420 --> 00:45:12.720
but you are not criticizing the worker. You earn&nbsp;
enrollment on the change you seek to make. Right?&nbsp;

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:19.140
That the original Mac was built by 13 people&nbsp;
at a pirate flag and they worked harder than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:24.300
anybody in Silicon Valley has ever worked before&nbsp;
or since. And they didn't get paid very much. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:29.160
if you talk to Susan years and years and years&nbsp;
later, I don't know if she's been on the podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:35.640
but she's great. She will talk about it&nbsp;
as being a seminal moment in her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:43.080
right? Because they got something done.
So that's why we need to have the aside about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.460 --> 00:45:51.900
freelancers and scale. Freelancing is magic. I&nbsp;
am a freelancer. I used to be a freelancer. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:59.400
between, I was an entrepreneur. They are different&nbsp;
jobs. Freelancers get paid when they work. They do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:04.920
the thing and it has their name or something like&nbsp;
it on it. It's very hard to scale freelance work.&nbsp;

00:46:04.920 --> 00:46:09.900
What you need to do is strip away the busy&nbsp;
work, outsource it. And what you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:14.700
do is get better clients, because better&nbsp;
clients demand better work and pay you more.&nbsp;

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:21.060
But what usually happens when a freelancer&nbsp;
starts to succeed is they hire junior versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:26.760
of themselves and try to push those people to&nbsp;
read their mind, work ever harder and faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:31.800
for less money than they get paid and then pass&nbsp;
it off as their work. That's super stressful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:38.040
it almost never works. And so if you really are&nbsp;
building something bigger than the two of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:38.040 --> 00:46:44.040
I would say, tell me this thing you were&nbsp;
building and why some customers will pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:50.340
more for it because you added human value.&nbsp;
Because if you're trying to out-Amazon Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:57.060
you've got trouble. Even Walmart can't out-Amazon&nbsp;
Amazon. That's not a race you can or want to win.&nbsp;

00:46:57.660 --> 00:47:02.820
So what we see is if someone is going to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:12.180
a bakery, or a wedding services business, or&nbsp;
a physical therapy facility, they can win by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:17.700
racing to the top. By saying, there are people&nbsp;
here who do work you cannot find anywhere else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:22.800
But do not expect that you're also going to get&nbsp;
that work faster and cheaper than you can get at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:28.080
other places, because you can't have everything.&nbsp;
And if you could make a promise to your customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:36.540
and your employees can see their contribution&nbsp;
to that promise, you're not going to have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:40.620
trouble at all, getting your employees to do&nbsp;
what they need to, because you created the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:46.320
conditions for better. You didn't try to manage&nbsp;
your way into getting them to give you a bargain.&nbsp;

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:54.840
Tim Ferriss: So let me take a step off the&nbsp;
highway onto the footpath, the bike path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.840 --> 00:48:00.420
on the side for a second, because this&nbsp;
is related and very self-interested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.420 --> 00:48:04.560
but I'll ask it anyway since I guess this&nbsp;
whole podcast is pretty self-interested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.560 --> 00:48:11.280
I get to have conversations with people I like&nbsp;
and respect. But you've known me for a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:15.840
now. I would say I think that's a fair way to put&nbsp;
it. We've known each other for a decent stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:25.740
and I consider you very good at being aware of&nbsp;
your own assumptions, the constraints that you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:31.380
applied to your life, choosing the rules,&nbsp;
choosing for instance, a story about money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.380 --> 00:48:38.280
past a certain point, past the requirements, past&nbsp;
your basic needs and wants. Money is a story, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.280 --> 00:48:44.580
choose a story you can be happy with, et cetera.
Is there anything you've observed over time with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.580 --> 00:48:51.960
me where you're like, "It's funny, Tim has this&nbsp;
conceptual limitation stuck between his teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:57.060
all the time and he can't seem to see it when he&nbsp;
looks in the mirror. He's always got that spinach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.060 --> 00:49:01.980
between those two teeth and it bothers him. He&nbsp;
is always picking at it, but he can't quite get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:07.740
it loose." This is a very long-winded way of&nbsp;
asking what are some of the maybe questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.740 --> 00:49:14.220
or issues that you seem come up repeatedly&nbsp;
with me? Is there anything that comes to mind?&nbsp;

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:22.980
Seth Godin: I was completely wrong about my first&nbsp;
impression of Tim Ferriss. And that's because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:31.740
based it on people who didn't understand what you&nbsp;
were trying to do that to me. And so I was getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.740 --> 00:49:37.500
hustled by people who said that you were the&nbsp;
person who was teaching them how to hustle and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:44.460
didn't bother to spend the time to understand what&nbsp;
you were actually doing. And that's completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.460 --> 00:49:53.640
on me. And you have developed a voice and a&nbsp;
contribution to the culture and your community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.660 --> 00:50:02.040
faster and at a younger age than almost anybody&nbsp;
I've worked with and way faster than I did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:12.240
And I don't see any spinach in your teeth. I&nbsp;
think that it's unlikely that you knew 16 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:16.500
ago that you would be having conversations&nbsp;
like this, that you would've built this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:20.520
platform and leverage you have&nbsp;
to teach so many people things.&nbsp;

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:28.620
But at the same time when the opportunities have&nbsp;
arisen, you have been really smart about saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:31.800
"No, I don't want to be a judge on Shark&nbsp;
Tank," and, "No, I don't want to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:36.360
out how to hustle people to pay me on&nbsp;
Cameo." You're saying, "I have something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:42.780
to narrate here for people, whether or not I&nbsp;
know exactly what tomorrow's going to be like."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:50.220
And it's not easy to do that regularly at the&nbsp;
level that you do it with the amount that you put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:56.340
out. So I know you weren't fishing for me to say&nbsp;
this, but I've needed to say it in public because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.340 --> 00:51:02.040
in private, in my head for the first nine months&nbsp;
that I sort of knew you, I didn't really know you.&nbsp;

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:09.780
Tim Ferriss: I appreciate that, Seth, and I&nbsp;
should provide a little context for folks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.780 --> 00:51:15.900
And the context is, when we have dinner&nbsp;
together on the East Coast, or I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:20.460
it's always on the East Coast, because you're&nbsp;
very good at setting your geographic bounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:29.580
I ask you for a lot of advice and you're very&nbsp;
forgiving and tolerant of me asking many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.580 --> 00:51:36.720
many questions, which I really deeply appreciate.&nbsp;
So that question came from a genuine place and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:44.460
thanks for the very kind words. I love doing&nbsp;
this. I love having these conversations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:51.840
there's absolutely no way I ever could have&nbsp;
foreseen this being my "job" 16 years ago. No way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:56.940
absolutely not. And I think probably four or&nbsp;
five years from now, whatever I am doing, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.780 --> 00:52:02.280
I will have the same statement for "There's&nbsp;
no way I could have predicted now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:08.040
I would be doing that five years from now."
Seth Godin: And let's just — to broaden that out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.640 --> 00:52:14.880
parents who have a sticker on the back of their&nbsp;
car have been indoctrinated to indoctrinate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:20.340
their kids to do the same thing. To say, "Oh,&nbsp;
you studied mechanical engineering, so you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:24.780
going to be a mechanical engineer for the next&nbsp;
70 years and then you'll die." And that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:31.560
what humans are capable of. What a joy to be able&nbsp;
to say, "I have no idea what I will be saying and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.560 --> 00:52:36.768
doing in five years." What a privilege that is.
Tim Ferriss: It is. I feel really fortunate.&nbsp;

00:52:36.768 --> 00:52:39.000
Seth Godin: Can we talk about&nbsp;
false proxies for a minute?&nbsp;

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.808
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about false&nbsp;
proxies. I'll let you lead the way.&nbsp;

00:52:41.808 --> 00:52:47.700
Seth Godin: Okay, so people might be wondering&nbsp;
about the wooden tile. So I have a 70 watt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:54.180
laser cutter in my basement. Doesn't everybody?&nbsp;
And so I make these things that are like I Ching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.180 --> 00:53:00.300
cards. And so instead of having notes or&nbsp;
something, I just really like the texture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:06.120
them. So that's what the wooden tiles are. And so&nbsp;
there's a wooden tile right here that says "False&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:12.240
proxies." We need proxies when we go shopping at&nbsp;
the supermarket, because if you're going to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:18.060
ketchup, you're not allowed to taste the ketchup&nbsp;
before you buy the ketchup. So the label on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.060 --> 00:53:24.360
bottle is a proxy for what's inside of it. And&nbsp;
we're even capable, as consumers, of figuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.360 --> 00:53:31.260
out we haven't had this particular flavor of salad&nbsp;
dressing before, but we can guess from the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.260 --> 00:53:35.580
clues that it's going to be good or not good.
And we use proxies to pick which restaurant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.580 --> 00:53:40.260
go to, and proxies to figure out what book to&nbsp;
buy, because you're not going to read the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:44.880
until after you buy it. Judging a book by its&nbsp;
cover is a very common and important thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:53.160
At work we developed proxies because we have&nbsp;
to hire people for a 20-, 30-, 40-year career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:58.140
before they work for us. So one proxy is, "Did&nbsp;
you go to a famous college?" One proxy is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.140 --> 00:54:04.200
"Are there any typos on your resume?" One proxy&nbsp;
is, "Are you good at interviewing?" But of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.200 --> 00:54:09.960
unless you're hiring someone to be an interviewer,&nbsp;
being good at interviewing is a false proxy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:18.420
And yeah, the thing about false proxies is they&nbsp;
lead to caste systems, to social stratification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:25.560
to prejudice, to misogyny, because we are quickly&nbsp;
making decisions on who to swipe left or right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:32.040
based on clues that aren't actually related&nbsp;
to whether the person can do the job or not.&nbsp;

00:54:32.040 --> 00:54:35.760
If someone is in a wheelchair, they&nbsp;
might be a great programmer. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:40.260
or not they're in a wheelchair is irrelevant&nbsp;
to whether they're a great programmer and yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:48.060
it's 2023 and it's still happening. So there's&nbsp;
all of this information that's now available to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:54.240
where we can look at the work instead of looking&nbsp;
at the proxies. So the simple solution, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.780 --> 00:54:58.740
I've been lucky enough to adopt, is I won't work&nbsp;
with someone unless I've worked with them before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.400 --> 00:55:04.560
And that means I will pay someone to do a short&nbsp;
project and I pay them whether they do a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:09.900
job or not. But if they're good at the project&nbsp;
now, I know that they're good at the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.900 --> 00:55:17.100
because they did the project. And with all&nbsp;
of the stuff that's happening in our world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:23.340
with the rate of change that's going on, with&nbsp;
the need for people — not who know something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.340 --> 00:55:27.840
they learned at school, because they can look&nbsp;
it up online, at Stack Overflow in two minutes.&nbsp;

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:33.780
What we need are people who are resilient, and&nbsp;
risk-taking, and honest, and transparent, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:40.620
connected, and loyal, and all these other words&nbsp;
which don't match up with the proxies we usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:47.280
use when we decide who to hire, who to let into an&nbsp;
institution, who to reward, who to follow online.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.940 --> 00:55:53.280
So I think it's important to just name it,&nbsp;
there are false proxies in our life and they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.280 --> 00:56:00.120
expensive. They get in the way of our output and&nbsp;
they also steal our soul and they denature our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.120 --> 00:56:06.180
culture, because when a proxy becomes important,&nbsp;
when for example, a celebrity gets the benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:12.000
of the doubt, what we've done is we've handed this&nbsp;
person leverage that isn't really helping anybody.&nbsp;

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:18.000
Tim Ferriss: Did I hear you correctly,&nbsp;
Seth? And if so, I'd love to hear you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:22.680
expand on this a bit, that you only work with&nbsp;
people you've worked with before. Did I hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:27.540
that correctly or was that a different line?
Seth Godin: What I mean is if I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:35.640
do a project, the old way to do&nbsp;
it was to look at the 10 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:41.820
viable people, pick the one who has the shiniest&nbsp;
set of easily measured proxies, and hire them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:48.900
And I have hired hundreds of people that way. And&nbsp;
for a while I told myself I was really good at it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.900 --> 00:56:52.980
And then I thought about it and I realized I just&nbsp;
thought I was really good at it, because of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:56.940
the people I didn't hire, because they didn't&nbsp;
match my perception of what I was looking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:03.540
And what I learned, particularly working on The&nbsp;
Carbon Almanac, is everyone was a volunteer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.540 --> 00:57:11.160
Everyone got the keys, take the car for a drive,&nbsp;
and if you did something that was really good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:15.780
we asked you to do more. And if you&nbsp;
didn't, we asked you to do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.780 --> 00:57:22.740
different, because the work was the point.
And so now I'm working on this interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:30.120
software pilot thing and I went to people who I've&nbsp;
seen do projects and who I've danced with before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:35.940
not based on where they live. One lived in&nbsp;
Nigeria, one lives in London, one lives in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:35.940 --> 00:57:42.180
Portland. "I've seen your work. I don't know you&nbsp;
personally. The personal stuff is an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.180 --> 00:57:50.160
proxy, but it doesn't usually match. So let's&nbsp;
dance again." And once we can seek enrollment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:55.920
once we can say to the world, "I'm looking for&nbsp;
a freelancer who wants to make this change in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.920 --> 00:58:01.020
the world, this project is this size and I'm&nbsp;
going to pay you this much to do it," we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:07.080
use our human judgment to decide who to offer the&nbsp;
project to. But then when the project comes back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:13.200
this one-hour, one-week project, pay them&nbsp;
fairly and then decide based on the work product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:22.020
whether it's useful. This is exactly the way&nbsp;
Matt builds WordPress. That Automattic is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:25.260
reading and writing culture where if you're&nbsp;
good at reading and writing, you get to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.260 --> 00:58:29.880
more stuff. But there's no points for being&nbsp;
good in a Zoom meeting. No one gets promoted.&nbsp;

00:58:29.880 --> 00:58:35.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, no one, definitely with Matt,&nbsp;
no one gets promoted for Zoom meetings and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.400 --> 00:58:39.060
do have to do some customer service, which&nbsp;
I think is actually a fantastic practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:44.040
Even if you're C-suite, you get to spend a&nbsp;
couple of weeks answering customer tickets.&nbsp;

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:52.380
Question for you about employee retention,&nbsp;
and I'd love to hear you speak to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:56.160
personal experience. You've hired many&nbsp;
people, and this may not map directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:56.160 --> 00:59:01.560
to the writing or the principles in The Song&nbsp;
of Significance — it may. But I'm wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:07.980
because hiring is one thing, retaining is&nbsp;
a close cousin, but not exactly the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.520 --> 00:59:13.920
And there are people out there, I know quite a few&nbsp;
people who are really good at putting in the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:14.940 --> 00:59:22.980
to hire really good people, but they haven't&nbsp;
been able to, for whatever reason, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:29.280
thoroughly, as effectively, think about retention.&nbsp;
So they lose some people who are very, very good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:35.640
What have you found to be, in your&nbsp;
experience, or observing others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:41.520
some of the key ingredients or possible approaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:48.000
to ensuring that you retain high performers?
Seth Godin: So if we look back just 50 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:48.660 --> 00:59:54.360
the purpose of a company town is if you are the&nbsp;
landlord, it's much harder for someone to quit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.020 --> 01:00:00.240
That the goal of the old school industrialist&nbsp;
is that your employees have no options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:02.940
because if they have no options, you&nbsp;
don't have to pay them very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:09.360
Let's compare that to an ad agency. I read&nbsp;
a book about them many years ago called St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:13.920
Luke's in London. There were 30 people, they&nbsp;
won a whole bunch of awards. When you win a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:18.300
bunch of awards as an ad agency, what happens&nbsp;
is you get a whole bunch of fancy new clients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:23.280
which means you have to hire more people, which&nbsp;
means the average goes down and then you get big&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:27.480
enough to, you've exhausted and you sell out&nbsp;
to Saatchi &amp; Saatchi, and then you are done.&nbsp;

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:32.100
And the 30 of them looked at each other&nbsp;
and they said, "We don't want to do that."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:32.760 --> 01:00:39.300
So they made a rule and the rule is "We're never&nbsp;
going to be more than 30 people." They then said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:44.940
to their clients, "That means we can't take&nbsp;
any new clients unless we lose an old client.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:49.560
So they have a waiting list of clients and&nbsp;
we have a waiting list of clients. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.560 --> 01:00:52.920
go to your old clients with your best&nbsp;
ideas and if they give you a hard time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.920 --> 01:00:56.340
you can say 'You're fired,' because I've&nbsp;
got another client ready to take your place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.020 --> 01:01:02.820
And it also means that if you are only going to&nbsp;
have 30 employees, if someone wants to leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:02.820 --> 01:01:04.860
let them leave." There's a waiting&nbsp;
list of people who want to work there.&nbsp;

01:01:05.880 --> 01:01:11.400
Turnover is a good thing when we are&nbsp;
doing human work, not a bad thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.400 --> 01:01:16.260
And what I would do if I was running&nbsp;
a real company is I would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:21.360
the first thing you've got to do on your first&nbsp;
day is update your LinkedIn page and keep it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:27.120
to date. And we're going to have a resume job&nbsp;
finding seminar every two weeks here. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:31.200
want you to stay here because you can't get&nbsp;
a better job. I want you to stay here because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:35.940
the conditions we've created, the work we&nbsp;
are doing is worth you staying here for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:44.340
And then I would listen. If I'm not creating&nbsp;
the conditions where the people who I need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.340 --> 01:01:51.780
be dancing with want to stay, I have to change the&nbsp;
conditions, not curse the people who are leaving.&nbsp;

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:59.640
And this permeability is where the future lies.&nbsp;
We don't have 40-year careers anymore. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.640 --> 01:02:05.580
four-month careers or three-year careers. So&nbsp;
onboarding is faster because you can say to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:05.580 --> 01:02:10.200
somebody, "Read everything in Slack, come&nbsp;
to work tomorrow, ready to start, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:15.600
it's all there." So now instead of onboarding&nbsp;
you in a month and a half, I onboarded you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:22.560
three hours. And because we're project-oriented,&nbsp;
turnover is a little different. And if I can keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:22.560 --> 01:02:29.880
somebody here for a long time, it's because they&nbsp;
want to stay here. This enrollment is the key.&nbsp;

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:35.880
Mahan Khalsa has written a book called Let's Get&nbsp;
Real or Let's Not Play about B2B selling. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:35.880 --> 01:02:40.080
the lesson of Let's Get Real or Let's Not Play&nbsp;
is simple. What promises are you going to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:45.000
this week? Promises about your career, promises&nbsp;
about your learning, promises to our customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:45.600 --> 01:02:50.700
Show us your work. We will relentlessly make it&nbsp;
better. This is how we're going to treat each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.700 --> 01:02:55.200
other. If this is the right place for you, I hope&nbsp;
you'll stay. And if it's not the right place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:55.200 --> 01:03:01.740
you, I hope we can agree that it's not, but&nbsp;
seeking retention doesn't feel right to me.&nbsp;

01:03:01.740 --> 01:03:08.340
So let me just elaborate on this book, because&nbsp;
it's overlooked and so beautiful. If you are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:08.340 --> 01:03:14.880
business-to-business selling expensive,&nbsp;
committed items to large organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:20.220
large organizations can now find out everything&nbsp;
they need without calling a salesperson.&nbsp;

01:03:21.360 --> 01:03:30.300
So the intelligent, trained, expensive salesperson&nbsp;
benefits by saying to the prospect on the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.300 --> 01:03:35.580
day, "Look, you've told me you have this big&nbsp;
problem you need to solve. You have a five million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.580 --> 01:03:41.760
assembly line that's letting you down, blah&nbsp;
blah. If we can solve this problem together, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:48.660
you ready to install our system? Because if it's&nbsp;
not real, let's not play. Don't waste my time, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:48.660 --> 01:03:51.720
won't waste yours. You're not going to buy from me&nbsp;
because I'm going to take you to the golf course.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:56.340
You're not going to buy from me because our RFP is&nbsp;
going to come in cheaper than somebody else's. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:56.340 --> 01:04:01.080
want my valuable time? I'm going to engage with&nbsp;
you, and tell you the truth and you'll tell me the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:01.080 --> 01:04:06.000
truth. You're going to draw your org chart for me.&nbsp;
You're going to tell me other complicated products&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:11.100
you've bought and why your company bought them.&nbsp;
And I'm going to get you promoted by teaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:11.100 --> 01:04:16.380
you how to buy the thing that's going to save your&nbsp;
assembly line. Let's get real or let's not play."&nbsp;

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:23.880
That kind of selling is how you sell a $10 million&nbsp;
product. You don't do it by spamming people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:23.880 --> 01:04:30.540
pretending you know their favorite color.
Tim Ferriss: Yes, very true. This is truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:34.740
Long ago in a former life when I had a&nbsp;
lot more hair and was just out of college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:35.400 --> 01:04:42.120
was selling to CEOs and CTOs and you do not sell&nbsp;
expensive systems by guessing their favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.120 --> 01:04:47.580
color or even having that conversation. In&nbsp;
fact, it'll be really counterproductive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.660 --> 01:04:56.040
The topic of sales makes me think of Zig&nbsp;
Ziglar. And I'm wondering, just out of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:56.040 --> 01:05:03.600
own personal curiosity, were there things that&nbsp;
come to mind where you disagreed with Zig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:03.600 --> 01:05:10.020
maybe you respected his position or a&nbsp;
principle he had, but you personally did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.780 --> 01:05:21.000
embrace whatever it was that he used as part&nbsp;
of his own script for navigating the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:26.580
people. Is there anything that you disagreed on?
Seth Godin: Many things, and we did it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.580 --> 01:05:32.340
friends. So it's funny, Zig has come up&nbsp;
twice today before you brought him up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:32.340 --> 01:05:37.020
which is weird. I sent a picture of him holding&nbsp;
one of my books to someone just four hours ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:45.600
Zig was my teacher from afar for a long time.&nbsp;
I memorized more than 72 hours of his tapes. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:51.960
listened to the selling tapes until they wore out.&nbsp;
And then I bought another set and I saw him in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:56.220
person a bunch of times and each time wrote him&nbsp;
a letter afterwards and each time he wrote back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:56.220 --> 01:06:01.680
and my letter would highlight the things that&nbsp;
worked and talk about the things that didn't. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:02.940 --> 01:06:08.040
one of the highlights of my speaking career was&nbsp;
when he and I shared a stage in front of 22,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:08.040 --> 01:06:13.380
people. It was pretty cool. And then I got to&nbsp;
publish his Goals book toward the end of his life.&nbsp;

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:22.320
Zig and I disagreed about astrology&nbsp;
and yoga because he was coming at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:22.320 --> 01:06:29.220
from a different cultural point of view from&nbsp;
Yazoo City, Mississippi. And we were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.220 --> 01:06:34.980
"Okay, we can disagree about stuff like that and&nbsp;
organized religion and things like that." What was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:42.840
interesting is when my understanding of permission&nbsp;
marketing evolved and Zig's key sentence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:47.280
you can get everything in life you want if you'll&nbsp;
just help enough other people get what they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:47.280 --> 01:06:55.080
kept coming up, because he meant it two ways and&nbsp;
one of the ways didn't work for me. He would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:55.080 --> 01:06:58.440
"You can get everything in life you want if you&nbsp;
help enough other people get what they want," as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.440 --> 01:07:04.440
a way of encouraging self-interested, short-term&nbsp;
people to see that the best way to get ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:12.780
was to do a favor for someone else because that&nbsp;
forced empathy opened the door to making a sale.&nbsp;

01:07:13.620 --> 01:07:21.840
And what shifted for me over time was how&nbsp;
about you can get everything in life you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.620 --> 01:07:29.580
and you can help other people get what they&nbsp;
want, but they're not one gets you the other one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:29.580 --> 01:07:34.620
That what we have is the chance to hold&nbsp;
open the door and let somebody else go in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:34.620 --> 01:07:39.660
not because we want them to give us something&nbsp;
in return, but simply because holding open the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.660 --> 01:07:46.740
door is, in itself, a significant useful&nbsp;
act that will fuel our next cycle of work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:47.820 --> 01:07:54.540
And we talked about it a little bit toward the&nbsp;
end of his life, but what Zig did for a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.540 --> 01:08:03.300
folks is help them become professional instead&nbsp;
of just hustling. And I also learned a lot about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:09.840
living the life of the professional speaker, and I&nbsp;
could go on and on, but if your listeners haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.840 --> 01:08:15.656
listened to a bunch of Zig, yes, it's dated, but&nbsp;
you can find the good stuff and be glad you did.&nbsp;

01:08:15.656 --> 01:08:20.940
Tim Ferriss: Where would you suggest people&nbsp;
start or what would you suggest they search for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:20.940 --> 01:08:27.900
Because the universe of Zig, as you mentioned,&nbsp;
62 hours is a starting point. Where might you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.900 --> 01:08:31.980
suggest they start? What should they look for?
Seth Godin: The stories in the book Secrets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.980 --> 01:08:37.740
Closing the Sale are gold. I could tell&nbsp;
word for word the story of the overalls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:44.340
And his original classic See You at&nbsp;
the Top, also filled with stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.720 --> 01:08:53.040
When I was starting out, I failed for seven years&nbsp;
in a row. It was a very long slog. And his tapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:59.100
which you can still get today on CD, also&nbsp;
filled with nothing but stories, were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.700 --> 01:09:05.400
super dated even then, but so cheesy and&nbsp;
wonderful that if you listen for an hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:05.400 --> 01:09:10.560
a day, you can't help but not quit.
Tim Ferriss: And to give us an image&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:14.640
in time. What were you failing at over&nbsp;
that period of time as you listened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.640 --> 01:09:18.720
these audio cassettes and wore them out?
Seth Godin: So my first job was in 1983&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:19.620 --> 01:09:25.980
at a software company in Boston. I worked&nbsp;
with Arthur C. Clarke, Michael Crichton, Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:25.980 --> 01:09:31.500
Bradbury. I was a brand manager. I was 24 years&nbsp;
old. It was spectacular. And then I left there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:36.000
moved to New York and started my own gig and&nbsp;
just kept failing at the book business. Chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:42.120
Conley and I did our first book together and then&nbsp;
I got 800 rejection letters in a row. That meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:46.560
every day I opened the mailbox and three people&nbsp;
had taken the time to write me a letter with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:46.560 --> 01:09:51.240
name on it and put a stamp on it saying, "We don't&nbsp;
like your project and we don't like you either."&nbsp;

01:09:51.840 --> 01:09:59.820
And it's very easy in the face that, and then I&nbsp;
was just pitching and pitching and pitching and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:59.820 --> 01:10:07.200
often in a way that amused me but was selfish.&nbsp;
And I was pitching to people whose job was to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:11.880
pitched. I wasn't calling people up on the phone&nbsp;
at home, right? This was the head of acquisitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.880 --> 01:10:20.880
at Simon and Schuster, whatever it was. But when&nbsp;
the rejections feel personal, if you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:20.880 --> 01:10:27.420
a way to keep caring, but understand that what&nbsp;
they're rejecting is the work, not the person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:34.680
it's super easy to give up. And that was the&nbsp;
transition that I needed to make. You didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:34.680 --> 01:10:40.380
like this idea. It's not that you didn't like&nbsp;
me. What can I learn from your criticism of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:46.680
the idea so that the next one will be better?
Tim Ferriss: You strike me as someone who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.680 --> 01:10:53.760
quite happy to have solo time, engage in solo&nbsp;
projects, maybe a project with a family member&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:58.500
or something else like building canoes. That&nbsp;
could be a whole conversation in and of itself.&nbsp;

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:07.500
How are you building? Are you building a community&nbsp;
orchestra for yourself at the moment or looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:07.500 --> 01:11:14.400
forward in the short term to doing that? Or I&nbsp;
should say in the soon-to-be-present future?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:15.480 --> 01:11:21.420
And if so, how are you approaching it? Because&nbsp;
this is something that I've also noticed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:21.420 --> 01:11:26.220
and this is not a brilliant insight,&nbsp;
but it seems to alleviate a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:32.220
existential dread and mitigate some&nbsp;
of the nihilism that people feel if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:32.220 --> 01:11:36.420
they simply have some type of shared purpose&nbsp;
or activity with a few people consistently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.620 --> 01:11:42.360
How are you thinking about that for yourself?
Seth Godin: This is just me. I don't know how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:49.200
broaden this to everybody. So the reputation I had&nbsp;
from the beginning was, I don't miss deadlines. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:53.100
never go over budget, and I'm going to deliver&nbsp;
you what I said I was going to deliver you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:53.820 --> 01:12:01.440
And no one who has worked for me has ever been&nbsp;
part of a layoff. People have lost their jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:01.440 --> 01:12:08.460
because it wasn't a good fit. But I've never said,&nbsp;
"All you guys, we're downsizing." When Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:09.780 --> 01:12:15.540
shut down Squidoo in one day with no&nbsp;
warning and no honest explanation as to why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:23.400
my eight coworkers and I all went down together.&nbsp;
But the promises I make I make very seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.120 --> 01:12:30.180
So that's good and it's bad. It means&nbsp;
that I hesitate to make big promises&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.780 --> 01:12:37.020
that might create the possibility that the big&nbsp;
thing is going to happen. But on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:37.020 --> 01:12:42.600
I can live with the confidence of knowing&nbsp;
that you can count on me to keep that promise.&nbsp;

01:12:42.600 --> 01:12:48.660
So when I think about what am I going&nbsp;
to do for my next five or 10 year gig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.860 --> 01:12:55.800
I think very hard about who am I promising&nbsp;
and what am I promising them? So I can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.800 --> 01:13:00.540
say to people what I used to say, which is "We're&nbsp;
renting this theater. Come and, for seven hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:04.920
I will be on stage answering questions. And by&nbsp;
the way, I'm organizing the whole thing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.920 --> 01:13:10.260
one volunteer and I'm going to serve you lunch."&nbsp;
That's what I used to do. And 400 people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.260 --> 01:13:16.500
come. It was thrilling to stand there for seven&nbsp;
hours and weave together all the answers. If I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.500 --> 01:13:21.360
announced I was going to do that, I could sell&nbsp;
it, but I couldn't keep that promise for sure.&nbsp;

01:13:21.360 --> 01:13:28.860
I don't want to do that because I don't want to,&nbsp;
as my career of a sudden has a different pace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:34.200
try to regain my ability to ski at&nbsp;
high speed, not what I'm trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.200 --> 01:13:40.680
do. So where is that sense of community? The&nbsp;
Carbon Almanac was that and continues to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.680 --> 01:13:46.020
but there's going to be another one. And I&nbsp;
think about whether I need it to be in person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:46.020 --> 01:13:51.900
or whether this future you and I have been&nbsp;
describing for so long is real and it won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.900 --> 01:13:56.880
be in person because I'm very comfortable engaging&nbsp;
with people who aren't sharing the same air as me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.260 --> 01:14:05.880
And most of all, is it worth doing for others?&nbsp;
Not, am I just here to entertain myself?&nbsp;

01:14:07.980 --> 01:14:12.720
Tim Ferriss: Seth, what else would you like&nbsp;
to talk about here? We can go in a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.720 --> 01:14:14.803
directions. What else do you have on here —
Seth Godin: Do you want to talk about —&nbsp;

01:14:14.803 --> 01:14:19.440
Tim Ferriss: — on your wooden tiles.
Seth Godin: Okay, so I want to tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.440 --> 01:14:25.020
story of the piano cover at the B conference, and&nbsp;
then I want to talk about meetings. Is that okay?&nbsp;

01:14:25.020 --> 01:14:29.100
Tim Ferriss: Sounds great.
Seth Godin: Okay. So I'm about to go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:33.480
stage in front of these entrepreneurs, all of whom&nbsp;
have raised far more money than I've ever raised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:33.480 --> 01:14:38.520
all of whom are young whippersnappers who&nbsp;
are going to save the planet. And I've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.520 --> 01:14:44.820
no slides or anything. I'm just there at this&nbsp;
beautiful winery north, it's like Yountville&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:47.460
or Booneville or something. And —
Tim Ferriss: This is in Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:48.420
or somewhere else?
Seth Godin: No, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:48.420 --> 01:14:54.540
I haven't been to Australia in a long time. This&nbsp;
is north of San Francisco and I need a shtick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.540 --> 01:15:02.820
I need some anecdote to get started. And off to&nbsp;
the side is a piano in one of those quilted piano&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.820 --> 01:15:04.860
covers. Can you visualize that?
Tim Ferriss: I can.&nbsp;

01:15:04.860 --> 01:15:08.340
Seth Godin: They have those special custom&nbsp;
made piano covers and on top of the piano&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.180 --> 01:15:13.860
is one of those little metal things that&nbsp;
says "Do not place anything on top of this."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:14.940 --> 01:15:20.880
And on top of that is the sign, but I can't&nbsp;
read the sign, so I walk over because that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:25.740
going to be my shtick, which is, "Did you ever&nbsp;
notice that people put a 'Do not…' and then put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.740 --> 01:15:35.160
a sign on top of that?" And on the sign that&nbsp;
says, "This is the original Steinway Grand D&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.160 --> 01:15:44.400
piano" — not an original, the original from 1884.
And I had my story, I went over and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:52.860
"The folks who built that Steinway in&nbsp;
1884 had no idea that 140 years later&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:53.700 --> 01:15:59.760
it would be working, it would be creating magic,&nbsp;
and we would be talking about it." And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.760 --> 01:16:06.060
"There's no guarantee that what we do is going&nbsp;
to be around in 140 years, but we could act like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.060 --> 01:16:14.820
it might be, we could say this thing we're doing&nbsp;
together is not to say, how do we raise as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.820 --> 01:16:21.840
money as we can? Or how do we get written up well&nbsp;
on this blog site or whatever. It's important."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.920 --> 01:16:27.900
And there are lots of kinds of important, it&nbsp;
could be important to one person, it could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:27.900 --> 01:16:35.400
important a century from now. But this idea that&nbsp;
the piano is still here, just it gave me chills.&nbsp;

01:16:36.480 --> 01:16:43.980
Tim Ferriss: I love that. The piano cover,&nbsp;
that is wild too. The original. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:43.980 --> 01:16:49.740
an experience just to be able to touch&nbsp;
something like that or be close to it. Wild.&nbsp;

01:16:49.740 --> 01:16:53.940
Seth Godin: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So meetings?&nbsp;

01:16:53.940 --> 01:17:00.180
Seth Godin: Meetings. I have been obsessed&nbsp;
with these for a long time and have lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:01.500 --> 01:17:06.180
a different path for meetings.&nbsp;
Our friend Tobi at Shopify&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.080 --> 01:17:13.800
wrote a script that deleted every group meeting&nbsp;
that was regularly scheduled in the whole company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.520 --> 01:17:19.200
and then the next day sent an email to everyone&nbsp;
in the company and said, "I just bought you back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:19.920 --> 01:17:25.800
your day." Because if you have 10,000 employees,&nbsp;
and there are all these weekly or daily meetings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:25.800 --> 01:17:30.900
we're talking about tens of millions of&nbsp;
dollars of wasted time. But on top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.900 --> 01:17:37.560
we're talking about the innovating brain&nbsp;
deadening idea of watching someone talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:37.560 --> 01:17:42.300
because they couldn't care enough to&nbsp;
take the time to send a memo instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:42.840 --> 01:17:49.620
or couldn't care enough to just record a 10-minute&nbsp;
video and send it to everyone to watch as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.620 --> 01:17:54.240
times as they wanted, slower, faster, read&nbsp;
the transcript and then get back to them.&nbsp;

01:17:54.960 --> 01:17:59.160
And Tobi said, "If you really need the meeting,&nbsp;
go ahead and put it back on your calendar. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:59.160 --> 01:18:05.760
welcome." And what meetings have become. So&nbsp;
Zoom is a miracle because Zoom eliminates&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:05.760 --> 01:18:10.680
geography when it comes to being able to connect&nbsp;
with people. And memos were a miracle because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.680 --> 01:18:18.000
they eliminated time, they're asynchronous. And&nbsp;
when we add video to this, what we get is either&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:18.780 --> 01:18:22.380
we're going to have everyone get&nbsp;
together for an hour so I can talk at you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:28.560
and I can take attendance based on you sitting&nbsp;
there. And what we know from surveys is people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:28.560 --> 01:18:36.000
hate this. It's one of the things they hate the&nbsp;
most about their day. And it's a form of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:44.520
authority, status, and control. So conversations&nbsp;
need to happen. More meetings need to happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:44.520 --> 01:18:49.320
never. You can have conversations all day. You&nbsp;
can have a two-minute conversation, a six-minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.320 --> 01:18:53.640
conversation, talk to this person or that person.
But a conversation means there are questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:53.640 --> 01:18:57.480
and there are answers. People are talking to&nbsp;
each other. Everyone is changed. At the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:58.500 --> 01:19:04.320
a meeting is a delivery of information for&nbsp;
the convenience of the person who called it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:12.420
And I think it is possible. I know it is&nbsp;
possible. We never once had a meeting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.420 --> 01:19:16.320
whole time we built The Carbon Almanac. There&nbsp;
were times I sent a video to people and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:20.100
"This is what's up. Watch it if you want to." But&nbsp;
because we were in time zones around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.100 --> 01:19:24.420
a meeting wouldn't have made any sense. We had&nbsp;
things where I said, "I'm having office hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:24.420 --> 01:19:32.460
you can come ask questions," but I didn't do&nbsp;
that other thing. And it feels to me like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:32.460 --> 01:19:37.800
future of the resilient organization is a&nbsp;
lot closer to what Matt does at Automattic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:44.940
which is how do we build this asynchronous&nbsp;
geography free institution that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:44.940 --> 01:19:51.360
depend on me making stuff up while I'm on camera.
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. And for people who may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:51.360 --> 01:19:57.480
not have caught his name before, Matt Mullenweg&nbsp;
is his name. He's been on this podcast a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:57.480 --> 01:20:05.280
times, and he also has been part of a podcast.&nbsp;
And this is, I believe, Automattic produced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:05.280 --> 01:20:11.100
called Distributed. I may be getting the name&nbsp;
wrong, but it is entirely about distributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:11.100 --> 01:20:16.740
organizations in unorthodox ways of approaching&nbsp;
the type of thing that we're discussing right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:21.660
meetings or lack thereof, different means&nbsp;
of communication, et cetera. And he's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:22.560 --> 01:20:29.160
distributed first for a very, very, very long&nbsp;
time. So yeah, they were incredibly prepared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:29.160 --> 01:20:34.068
albeit coincidentally for everything&nbsp;
that happened during lockdown and so on.&nbsp;

01:20:34.068 --> 01:20:37.980
Seth Godin: Well, but coincidentally,&nbsp;
everyone else was unprepared. Have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.980 --> 01:20:43.607
seen what his team has built with ChatGPT&nbsp;
for blogs? Has he shown you the version yet?&nbsp;

01:20:43.607 --> 01:20:46.440
Tim Ferriss: I don't know if it's&nbsp;
public. I have seen screenshots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.440 --> 01:20:49.080
but yeah. Is it public? I don't know.
Seth Godin: It's not public, but we're only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:49.080 --> 01:20:53.100
talking, so we're allowed to talk.
Tim Ferriss: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:53.100 --> 01:20:57.000
go for it. So if you want to talk —
Seth Godin: I was going to say it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.000 --> 01:21:05.400
the single best use I have seen of ChatGPT or&nbsp;
whatever they're using, being implemented in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.400 --> 01:21:11.741
way where it's not just a talking llama,&nbsp;
it's actually, "Oh yeah, I need this."&nbsp;

01:21:11.741 --> 01:21:15.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, for sure. They&nbsp;
create some amazing products. So yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.240 --> 01:21:21.131
that'll be the teaser. The teaser for people.
Seth Godin: I'm just here for the hype.&nbsp;

01:21:21.131 --> 01:21:26.460
Tim Ferriss: There's good things to come. Right.&nbsp;
That's what I think when I say that, when I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.460 --> 01:21:33.408
South Dakota. Oh, Seth, is there anything else&nbsp;
you would like to chat about before we begin to —&nbsp;

01:21:33.408 --> 01:21:39.540
Seth Godin: Your listeners love tips. I've got&nbsp;
one last tactic tip that changed the lives of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:39.540 --> 01:21:44.760
lot of people. And then we'll do the magical wrap&nbsp;
up stuff. We invented something called page 19&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:44.760 --> 01:21:50.820
thinking, and we knew there was going to be page&nbsp;
19 of the Almanac. It was going to be written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.820 --> 01:21:56.880
copy, edited, illustrated type, set, footnoted.&nbsp;
But there wasn't one person on our team who knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:56.880 --> 01:22:03.420
how to make page 19. And so we said to the team,&nbsp;
"We know in the future there will be a page 19.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:03.420 --> 01:22:08.100
We know that it will come from this group, but we&nbsp;
also know there's not anyone here who's qualified,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:08.100 --> 01:22:15.000
so what should we do?" And the answer is someone&nbsp;
should write a paragraph of page 19 and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:20.160
"Please make this better." And then someone else&nbsp;
will add to that and someone else will footnote&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:20.160 --> 01:22:24.480
that and someone else will illustrate that.
And we will relentlessly criticize page 19&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:24.480 --> 01:22:33.060
without one saying, "The person who worked on it&nbsp;
was wrong or incompetent." And page 19 thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.060 --> 01:22:39.180
I've always had page 19 thinking, I didn't need&nbsp;
it. But so many people when they heard this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:39.180 --> 01:22:46.680
felt the freedom to now speak up and contribute&nbsp;
because they knew it was going to get better. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.680 --> 01:22:52.140
if you think about it, that's Apple, that's Ford.&nbsp;
That's every company you can name, right? Sergey&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:52.140 --> 01:22:58.800
did not program the Google you are using today. It&nbsp;
started with a little, tiny German and someone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.800 --> 01:23:04.080
someone and someone and someone and someone, when&nbsp;
you feel stuck, just look for page 19 thinking.&nbsp;

01:23:04.080 --> 01:23:11.040
Tim Ferriss: And is the way to look for&nbsp;
page 19 thinking, to take the pressure off&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:11.040 --> 01:23:18.540
by lowering your expectations in a sense so&nbsp;
that you can iterate your way to excellence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:19.440 --> 01:23:27.480
Is this applicable to a solo shop or a very small&nbsp;
shop? Could you maybe elaborate on what you mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:27.480 --> 01:23:31.500
what it could mean, to embrace that?
Seth Godin: It puts the pressure on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:32.340 --> 01:23:36.900
It doesn't need the pressure off. It puts the&nbsp;
pressure on. Because you can no longer be a prima&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:36.900 --> 01:23:41.280
donna who says, "Ah, I don't, I'm afraid someone's&nbsp;
going to punch me in the nose." Because you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:41.280 --> 01:23:48.840
to say, "My nose is not involved." Right? And I&nbsp;
heard Danny's great story about my nose, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:49.380 --> 01:23:53.580
made me smile. He offered —
Tim Ferriss: This is Danny Meyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:53.580 --> 01:23:57.300
folks, for people who don't know.
Seth Godin: After he broke my nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.300 --> 01:24:02.820
he offered to straighten it out. And as you&nbsp;
can see in the video, it's still crooked. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:02.820 --> 01:24:09.780
once you understand that you live in a page 19&nbsp;
world, the pressure is on for you to put out work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.620 --> 01:24:17.160
that can generously be criticized. Don't ship&nbsp;
junk, not allowed, but create the conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:17.160 --> 01:24:23.280
for the thing you're noodling on to become&nbsp;
real. That doesn't happen by you hoarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:23.280 --> 01:24:30.000
it until it's perfect. It happens by you&nbsp;
creating a process for it to get better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:34.080
So now you're on the hook. Fish don't like to&nbsp;
be on the hook. People should be on the hook.&nbsp;

01:24:37.980 --> 01:24:43.140
Tim Ferriss: I love it. And I was thinking&nbsp;
of how this might apply to my own, and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:43.140 --> 01:24:47.400
since we last spoke, been writing fiction&nbsp;
and shipping fiction. I'm not saying it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:48.240 --> 01:24:57.540
Ursula K. Le Guin, but I'm, I'm happy with it. I&nbsp;
am on the hook and have tried to create, I haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:57.540 --> 01:25:04.380
found exactly what I think will be most helpful&nbsp;
later, but to create an environment in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:05.700 --> 01:25:10.620
perfect is not the enemy of good, but that&nbsp;
I also have a means of not shipping garbage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:10.620 --> 01:25:18.600
and actually having people review my work. How do&nbsp;
you like to solicit feedback on writing? This is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:18.600 --> 01:25:26.400
I know a tangent, but if you reach out to&nbsp;
people to proofread or review drafts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:26.400 --> 01:25:31.680
something you've put together, how do you&nbsp;
enable them to be the best proofreaders?&nbsp;

01:25:31.680 --> 01:25:35.880
Seth Godin: Three words, and then&nbsp;
some definitions. The three words are:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:35.880 --> 01:25:43.727
criticism, feedback, and advice. I'm terrible at&nbsp;
criticism. Don't like criticism. I'm okay with —&nbsp;

01:25:43.727 --> 01:25:46.920
Tim Ferriss: You're terrible at receiving it.
Seth Godin: Receiving it, yeah. I like criticizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:46.920 --> 01:25:49.440
people. Yeah, no problem. But&nbsp;
being criticized, not my thing.&nbsp;

01:25:51.480 --> 01:25:59.940
Good feedback is precious. But advice, advice&nbsp;
I can handle because advice is one human who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:59.940 --> 01:26:06.180
on the side of the other human helping them&nbsp;
turn on lights. Now, when it comes to writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.180 --> 01:26:13.620
there is line editing, copyediting, developmental&nbsp;
editing, and proofreading. They do not mean the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:13.620 --> 01:26:19.140
same thing. Proofreading means did the changes&nbsp;
that were instructed to be made get made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:20.100 --> 01:26:26.700
So proofreading is sort of trivial. Copyediting&nbsp;
says, "What do you think about the Oxford comma?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:26.700 --> 01:26:31.020
Copyediting says, "I don't like the&nbsp;
way you spelled this kind of thing."&nbsp;

01:26:31.020 --> 01:26:36.420
Line editing says this sentence would be&nbsp;
better if you moved the second half to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.420 --> 01:26:43.020
the first half that I'm going to smooth your&nbsp;
voice up. And developmental editing is magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.860 --> 01:26:49.920
I read your work. Let's just talk in general&nbsp;
about the change you seek to make and why it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.920 --> 01:26:55.440
could be made better in a different way. So&nbsp;
I work with the magical Nicki Papadopoulos at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:55.980 --> 01:27:02.280
Penguin mostly because her developmental&nbsp;
editing is the best I've ever encountered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:02.880 --> 01:27:08.160
She says "The title of the book is wrong and you&nbsp;
need to move these three chapters to the end."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.240 --> 01:27:14.760
What's that worth? That's spectacular. But&nbsp;
I will confess, if she's not listening, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:14.760 --> 01:27:22.620
before I send her a book, I send it to a paid copy&nbsp;
editor who works for me. I don't work for him.&nbsp;

01:27:22.620 --> 01:27:28.260
And for $1,000, he copyedits the book. So&nbsp;
he is not allowed to change any of my voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:28.260 --> 01:27:34.140
but he fixes every one of the little things.&nbsp;
So when I send it to Penguin, they think, "Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.140 --> 01:27:40.500
this guy's so good at all the detail." No,&nbsp;
I'm not, Dr. Aker is, but you don't know he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.500 --> 01:27:46.140
exists because I send it to him before I send&nbsp;
it to you. So the point is, you will run into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.140 --> 01:27:50.820
people who think that the most important thing&nbsp;
they can tell you is you're missing a comma in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:50.820 --> 01:27:58.320
the third line. This is not helpful. This work is&nbsp;
now cheap. It's free online. You can just upload&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.320 --> 01:28:03.780
a file and someone will fix it for you. But&nbsp;
developmental editors, they're priceless. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.780 --> 01:28:09.060
need to find someone who has that as a skill.
Opinions don't matter. That's why the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:09.060 --> 01:28:14.160
thing with logos — don't show anybody your&nbsp;
logo and don't tell anybody your new kid's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:14.160 --> 01:28:17.760
name before they're born, because everyone&nbsp;
thinks they have an opinion about a logo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.760 --> 01:28:23.280
but no one's got expertise. It's none of their&nbsp;
business. Pick your logo and do your logo. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:23.280 --> 01:28:28.440
same thing's true with your kid's name. And&nbsp;
when you show your writing to an amateur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:28.980 --> 01:28:35.040
they're going to give you logo advice. It's the&nbsp;
book isn't for them. You need to engage with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:35.040 --> 01:28:40.020
people who the book is for, watch what they do&nbsp;
with it. See what lights them up. So in my case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:40.740 --> 01:28:46.440
after Permission Marketing, hearing people say&nbsp;
back to me which parts of the book resonated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:46.440 --> 01:28:52.620
them taught me how to write the next book because&nbsp;
I didn't ask anybody, "How do I make this book&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:52.620 --> 01:28:58.140
better?" I just said, "I wrote this book," and&nbsp;
seeing what they did, I gave them more of that.&nbsp;

01:28:58.140 --> 01:29:04.080
Tim Ferriss: Right? By extension, the Zig&nbsp;
could have taken the fact that you know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:10.320
overall story word for word and be like, "Okay,&nbsp;
interesting. Let's take a look at why that worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.320 --> 01:29:13.680
and maybe I'll give him an extra&nbsp;
heaping dose of something like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:13.680 --> 01:29:17.280
overall story in the next book."
Seth Godin: Exactly. And I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:17.280 --> 01:29:22.920
for a fact that that's exactly what Zig did.
Tim Ferriss: So Seth, the new book is The Song of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:22.920 --> 01:29:28.944
Significance, subtitle, A New Manifesto for Teams.&nbsp;
People can find you at sethgodin.com, seth.blog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.240 --> 01:29:34.920
Is there anything else you would like to&nbsp;
add? Any requests you would like to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:34.920 --> 01:29:42.300
of my audience? Any complaints you'd like to&nbsp;
lodge formally, criticisms maybe, or anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.300 --> 01:29:48.900
else you would like to say before we wrap up?
Seth Godin: I am overwhelmed with gratitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.900 --> 01:29:56.880
that I get to do this, and I am feeling&nbsp;
the murmurs of generosity and abundance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:56.880 --> 01:30:05.520
replacing the small minded scarcity that was&nbsp;
so common for so long as people hustled their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.520 --> 01:30:11.220
way through one thing after another over the last&nbsp;
bunch of years. And I think as we all come out of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.220 --> 01:30:18.840
hopefully come out of this worldwide pandemic,&nbsp;
people are taking a deep breath and focusing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:19.380 --> 01:30:27.120
contribution and generosity. And all I can tell&nbsp;
you is I'm overwhelmed with gratitude for you and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.120 --> 01:30:33.887
for the people who support my work, just making&nbsp;
things better. Because if not that, what? So&nbsp;

01:30:33.887 --> 01:30:39.240
Tim Ferriss: Thank you. Thank you so much, Seth.&nbsp;
And to everybody listening, we'll have everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:39.960 --> 01:30:44.460
that we discussed in the show notes and&nbsp;
probably quite a bit more at tim.blog/podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:44.460 --> 01:30:49.320
where you can find this and show notes on&nbsp;
every other episode. And until next time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.320 --> 01:30:54.840
just to be a bit nicer than is necessary to&nbsp;
yourself and to other people, make things just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.840 --> 01:31:00.300
a bit better. Have that extra conversation. Say&nbsp;
that extra nice word. Do what you can. It doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:00.300 --> 01:31:04.140
have to be huge. Probably shouldn't be huge,&nbsp;
because then you can hide behind that as we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.140 --> 01:31:10.380
covered previous episodes. Make it small, make it&nbsp;
matter. And until next time, thanks for tuning in.

